{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgApVFHNKPkkTYa9c9NCn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014J4eMQpuv5avXvB86osgAe","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"pkill -f \"python3 server.py\"; cd /private/tmp/h-QFsRmD && cat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# notes-mcp: things real MCP clients will trip over\n\nChecked by running `python3 server.py` and sending raw JSON-RPC with curl (no server changes). Most important first. The server held only in-memory state, and I did not modify files other than this one.\n\n## 1. Auth failures are bare HTTP 401s (highest impact)\n- `add_note` without a token, and `resources/*` without a token, return `401 {\"error\":\"sign in required\"}` / `{\"error\":\"unauthorized\"}`.\n  - There is **no `WWW-Authenticate` header**.\n  - There is no OAuth protected-resource metadata. `GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` returns 405 `use POST`.\n  - The body is not JSON-RPC.\n- Clients treat a 401 as \"start the OAuth flow\", find no metadata, and report a generic auth or connection failure. Some mark the whole server as failed or needs-auth, so the open read tools become unusable too.\n- A tool-level 401 is wrong in a different way. The spec reserves HTTP 401 for transport authorization. A missing sign-in for one tool should be a normal result with `isError: true` and a clear message, or a JSON-RPC error.\n- The tool description says \"Needs a signed-in session\", but the only auth method is a static Bearer dev token. Nothing tells a client how to obtain it.\n- **`resources/list` is 401 for anonymous callers**, yet `initialize` advertises the `resources` capability. Clients such as Claude Code, Codex and Gemini call `resources/list` and `resources/templates/list` right after connecting. An anonymous client therefore gets a 401 during setup, even though README says \"Reads are open\".\n  - Either allow these methods anonymously, or don't advertise `resources`.\n- Related: `resources/read` ignores the requested `uri`. `notes://nope` returns the guide content instead of an error.\n\n## 2. Tool errors are protocol errors, not tool results\n- `get_note` with an unknown id returns JSON-RPC error `-32602 \"note not found\"`. Bad arguments also come back as `-32602`, and an unknown tool as `-32601`.\n- Errors that the model could act on should be `result: {isError: true, content: [...]}`. Many clients hide or flatten JSON-RPC errors, so the model never sees the message and can't retry with corrected input.\n- `-32601` for an unknown tool should be `-32602`.\n- Error text leaks Python internals, e.g. `invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'x'` for `limit:\"x\"`.\n\n## 3. `inputSchema` is declared but never enforced\nConstraints that the schema promises, and the server ignores:\n- `get_note.id` has `pattern ^n-[0-9]+$`. `id: 5` and `id: \"zzz\"` are accepted and fall through to \"not found\". `id: []` or an array of arguments is also not rejected cleanly.\n- `search_notes.limit` has `minimum: 1`. `limit:-1` is accepted and silently slices from the end (it returned 4 of 5 hits). `limit: 0` returns nothing. `limit` above 50 is accepted. A string `limit` gives the int() error above.\n- `search_notes.query` is required, but `{}` and `\"\"` return every note.\n- `add_note` with a missing `body` crashes (see #4). Empty `title` (`minLength: 1`) is accepted, and `add_note` accepts non-string values.\n- `search_notes.total` is the length after truncation by `limit`, not the real match count. That is misleading for paging.\n\n## 4. Crashes and dropped connections\nUnhandled exceptions kill the request with no HTTP response (curl gets an empty reply). A client sees a hang or reset, not an error:\n- `add_note` (authenticated) with a missing `title` or `body`: `KeyError`.\n- A JSON body of `null`, `\"x\"` or `42`: `AttributeError`.\n- Running `add_note` against an empty note set would also crash (`max()` of an empty sequence), and the id generation isn't thread-safe under `ThreadingHTTPServer`.\n- A JSON-RPC batch `[]` returns `[]`. The spec calls for an error. Batch handling also skips logging, and a batch ignores the auth/status of its items.\n\n## 5. `outputSchema` / `structuredContent` mismatches\n- `search_notes` declares `outputSchema: {\"type\":\"object\"}`, which is so loose it is useless. The real shape is `{hits:[…], total}`. Declare it, since strict clients validate it.\n- `get_note` and `add_note` have strict schemas, and the returned data matches them for existing notes. Fragile points:\n  - A note stored without `tags` or with an empty tag string breaks the `minLength: 1` constraint.\n  - `add_note` accepts any title, so a title that fails the schema can later fail `get_note` validation.\n  - SDK clients (the Python and TypeScript SDKs) validate `structuredContent` against `outputSchema` and throw on a mismatch.\n- Error results (`isError: true`) must not be validated against `outputSchema`. This server has none today, but fix #2 and keep that in mind.\n- The `text` content is a JSON dump of the structured data. That is fine, but the search snippet cuts words and can start mid-word (`\"re at 61C…\"`).\n\n## 6. Protocol and transport details\n- `notifications/*` other than `initialized` (e.g. `notifications/cancelled`, `notifications/roots/list_changed`) get a `200` with a `-32601` error and `id: null`. Notifications must get `202` with no body. Clients, notably ones sending `notifications/cancelled`, may log errors or break.\n- `GET /mcp` returns `405` with a JSON body but no `Allow` header. Clients that try to open the optional SSE stream (`Accept: text/event-stream`) tolerate 405, but add `Allow: POST, DELETE`.\n- Sessions:\n  - `Mcp-Session-Id` is issued at `initialize` but never stored or validated. A bogus id is echoed back as valid, an unknown or expired session never gets 404, and `DELETE` always returns 204.\n  - A stateless server is fine, but then it shouldn't pretend to issue sessions.\n  - The id is not issued on the non-initialize reply path for batches, which echo whatever came in.\n- `MCP-Protocol-Version` header is not validated. `1999-01-01` is accepted. `initialize` always answers `2025-06-18` regardless of what the client requested, which is allowed, but clients on older versions (e.g. 2024-11-05) will reject the answer.\n- `Content-Type` and `Accept` are not checked. The server always replies `application/json`, which is fine because JSON responses are allowed.\n- `Origin` is not validated. A request from `http://evil.example` succeeds. The server binds to 127.0.0.1, but without an Origin check a web page can still reach it by DNS rebinding. The spec requires Origin validation for local servers.\n- No CORS and no `OPTIONS`: it replies `501` with an HTML page, so browser-based clients (such as the MCP Inspector in the browser) can't connect.\n- `/mcp/` works but `/` is 404 (fine). Responses are HTTP/1.0 with `Connection: close`, so there is no keep-alive. That is OK for small use, but slower for chatty clients.\n- `initialize` does not check the `params`. A missing `protocolVersion` or `clientInfo` is silently accepted.\n- Per-method `tools/list` has no `title`, no pagination (fine for 3 tools), and `listChanged: false` is declared consistently.\n\n## 7. Smaller things\n- README's token (`dev-token-123`) is the default when `NOTES_TOKEN` is unset. If this is exposed beyond localhost, the default token is a public secret. The comparison is not constant-time, and `who` is only `dev` or `anon`.\n- `add_note` writes only to memory, so notes are lost on restart, and the tool says nothing about that. `readOnlyHint`/`destructiveHint` annotations are fine. Add `idempotentHint: false` and `openWorldHint: false` for completeness.\n- Logging: `ok` for a 401 or JSON-RPC error is logged as false, but batches and crashes are not logged at all. The log has no request id or session id.\n- Search is substring-only and case-insensitive, and `snippet` offsets are computed on the lowercased text. That is fine for ASCII, but can drift for characters whose lowercase form has a different length.\n\n## Suggested fix order\n1. Make anonymous `resources/*` work (or drop the capability), and stop returning HTTP 401 for tool-level auth. If you want real auth, return 401 with `WWW-Authenticate` and OAuth metadata.\n2. Turn tool failures into `isError: true` results with human-readable text.\n3. Validate arguments against the declared schemas and wrap `do_POST` in a try/except that returns a JSON-RPC `-32603`.\n4. Return 202 for every notification, and validate `Origin`, `MCP-Protocol-Version` and the session id.\nEOF\ngit status --short"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5658,"cache_read_input_tokens":28000,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5658},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1aee5fda-9a05-470c-967f-df2db9a4d62e","uuid":"528d9bae-c027-47ae-b7c0-9dfc6940698a","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:38:23.840Z","request_id":"req_011CgApVF2jM9PTQVGojRMtF","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014J4eMQpuv5avXvB86osgAe":{"command":"pkill -f \"python3 server.py\"; cd /private/tmp/h-QFsRmD && cat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# notes-mcp: things real MCP clients will trip over\n\nChecked by running `python3 server.py` and sending raw JSON-RPC with curl (no server changes). Most important first. The server held only in-memory state, and I did not modify files other than this one.\n\n## 1. Auth failures are bare HTTP 401s (highest impact)\n- `add_note` without a token, and `resources/*` without a token, return `401 {\"error\":\"sign in required\"}` / `{\"error\":\"unauthorized\"}`.\n  - There is **no `WWW-Authenticate` header**.\n  - There is no OAuth protected-resource metadata. `GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` returns 405 `use POST`.\n  - The body is not JSON-RPC.\n- Clients treat a 401 as \"start the OAuth flow\", find no metadata, and report a generic auth or connection failure. Some mark the whole server as failed or needs-auth, so the open read tools become unusable too.\n- A tool-level 401 is wrong in a different way. The spec reserves HTTP 401 for transport authorization. A missing sign-in for one tool should be a normal result with `isError: true` and a clear message, or a JSON-RPC error.\n- The tool description says \"Needs a signed-in session\", but the only auth method is a static Bearer dev token. Nothing tells a client how to obtain it.\n- **`resources/list` is 401 for anonymous callers**, yet `initialize` advertises the `resources` capability. Clients such as Claude Code, Codex and Gemini call `resources/list` and `resources/templates/list` right after connecting. An anonymous client therefore gets a 401 during setup, even though README says \"Reads are open\".\n  - Either allow these methods anonymously, or don't advertise `resources`.\n- Related: `resources/read` ignores the requested `uri`. `notes://nope` returns the guide content instead of an error.\n\n## 2. Tool errors are protocol errors, not tool results\n- `get_note` with an unknown id returns JSON-RPC error `-32602 \"note not found\"`. Bad arguments also come back as `-32602`, and an unknown tool as `-32601`.\n- Errors that the model could act on should be `result: {isError: true, content: [...]}`. Many clients hide or flatten JSON-RPC errors, so the model never sees the message and can't retry with corrected input.\n- `-32601` for an unknown tool should be `-32602`.\n- Error text leaks Python internals, e.g. `invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'x'` for `limit:\"x\"`.\n\n## 3. `inputSchema` is declared but never enforced\nConstraints that the schema promises, and the server ignores:\n- `get_note.id` has `pattern ^n-[0-9]+$`. `id: 5` and `id: \"zzz\"` are accepted and fall through to \"not found\". `id: []` or an array of arguments is also not rejected cleanly.\n- `search_notes.limit` has `minimum: 1`. `limit:-1` is accepted and silently slices from the end (it returned 4 of 5 hits). `limit: 0` returns nothing. `limit` above 50 is accepted. A string `limit` gives the int() error above.\n- `search_notes.query` is required, but `{}` and `\"\"` return every note.\n- `add_note` with a missing `body` crashes (see #4). Empty `title` (`minLength: 1`) is accepted, and `add_note` accepts non-string values.\n- `search_notes.total` is the length after truncation by `limit`, not the real match count. That is misleading for paging.\n\n## 4. Crashes and dropped connections\nUnhandled exceptions kill the request with no HTTP response (curl gets an empty reply). A client sees a hang or reset, not an error:\n- `add_note` (authenticated) with a missing `title` or `body`: `KeyError`.\n- A JSON body of `null`, `\"x\"` or `42`: `AttributeError`.\n- Running `add_note` against an empty note set would also crash (`max()` of an empty sequence), and the id generation isn't thread-safe under `ThreadingHTTPServer`.\n- A JSON-RPC batch `[]` returns `[]`. The spec calls for an error. Batch handling also skips logging, and a batch ignores the auth/status of its items.\n\n## 5. `outputSchema` / `structuredContent` mismatches\n- `search_notes` declares `outputSchema: {\"type\":\"object\"}`, which is so loose it is useless. The real shape is `{hits:[…], total}`. Declare it, since strict clients validate it.\n- `get_note` and `add_note` have strict schemas, and the returned data matches them for existing notes. Fragile points:\n  - A note stored without `tags` or with an empty tag string breaks the `minLength: 1` constraint.\n  - `add_note` accepts any title, so a title that fails the schema can later fail `get_note` validation.\n  - SDK clients (the Python and TypeScript SDKs) validate `structuredContent` against `outputSchema` and throw on a mismatch.\n- Error results (`isError: true`) must not be validated against `outputSchema`. This server has none today, but fix #2 and keep that in mind.\n- The `text` content is a JSON dump of the structured data. That is fine, but the search snippet cuts words and can start mid-word (`\"re at 61C…\"`).\n\n## 6. Protocol and transport details\n- `notifications/*` other than `initialized` (e.g. `notifications/cancelled`, `notifications/roots/list_changed`) get a `200` with a `-32601` error and `id: null`. Notifications must get `202` with no body. Clients, notably ones sending `notifications/cancelled`, may log errors or break.\n- `GET /mcp` returns `405` with a JSON body but no `Allow` header. Clients that try to open the optional SSE stream (`Accept: text/event-stream`) tolerate 405, but add `Allow: POST, DELETE`.\n- Sessions:\n  - `Mcp-Session-Id` is issued at `initialize` but never stored or validated. A bogus id is echoed back as valid, an unknown or expired session never gets 404, and `DELETE` always returns 204.\n  - A stateless server is fine, but then it shouldn't pretend to issue sessions.\n  - The id is not issued on the non-initialize reply path for batches, which echo whatever came in.\n- `MCP-Protocol-Version` header is not validated. `1999-01-01` is accepted. `initialize` always answers `2025-06-18` regardless of what the client requested, which is allowed, but clients on older versions (e.g. 2024-11-05) will reject the answer.\n- `Content-Type` and `Accept` are not checked. The server always replies `application/json`, which is fine because JSON responses are allowed.\n- `Origin` is not validated. A request from `http://evil.example` succeeds. The server binds to 127.0.0.1, but without an Origin check a web page can still reach it by DNS rebinding. The spec requires Origin validation for local servers.\n- No CORS and no `OPTIONS`: it replies `501` with an HTML page, so browser-based clients (such as the MCP Inspector in the browser) can't connect.\n- `/mcp/` works but `/` is 404 (fine). Responses are HTTP/1.0 with `Connection: close`, so there is no keep-alive. That is OK for small use, but slower for chatty clients.\n- `initialize` does not check the `params`. A missing `protocolVersion` or `clientInfo` is silently accepted.\n- Per-method `tools/list` has no `title`, no pagination (fine for 3 tools), and `listChanged: false` is declared consistently.\n\n## 7. Smaller things\n- README's token (`dev-token-123`) is the default when `NOTES_TOKEN` is unset. If this is exposed beyond localhost, the default token is a public secret. The comparison is not constant-time, and `who` is only `dev` or `anon`.\n- `add_note` writes only to memory, so notes are lost on restart, and the tool says nothing about that. `readOnlyHint`/`destructiveHint` annotations are fine. Add `idempotentHint: false` and `openWorldHint: false` for completeness.\n- Logging: `ok` for a 401 or JSON-RPC error is logged as false, but batches and crashes are not logged at all. The log has no request id or session id.\n- Search is substring-only and case-insensitive, and `snippet` offsets are computed on the lowercased text. That is fine for ASCII, but can drift for characters whose lowercase form has a different length.\n\n## Suggested fix order\n1. Make anonymous `resources/*` work (or drop the capability), and stop returning HTTP 401 for tool-level auth. If you want real auth, return 401 with `WWW-Authenticate` and OAuth metadata.\n2. Turn tool failures into `isError: true` results with human-readable text.\n3. Validate arguments against the declared schemas and wrap `do_POST` in a try/except that returns a JSON-RPC `-32603`.\n4. Return 202 for every notification, and validate `Origin`, `MCP-Protocol-Version` and the session id.\nEOF\ngit status --short"}}}
